Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let $(M_a)_{a\ge1}$ be bounded operators on $H$ with

$$\sum_{a=1}^NM_a^*M_a\longrightarrow I\qquad\text{strongly as }N\to\infty .$$

Then:

(a) $\sum_{a\ge1}\|M_ax\|^2=\|x\|^2$ for every $x\in H$;

(b) for every positive trace-class $\rho$ the series $\sum_{a\ge1}M_a\rho M_a^*$ converges in trace norm to a positive trace-class operator $\Phi(\rho)$ with $\operatorname{tr}\Phi(\rho)=\operatorname{tr}\rho$;

(c) $\Phi$ extends uniquely to a linear positive map $\Phi:\mathcal S_1(H)\to\mathcal S_1(H)$ with $\operatorname{tr}\Phi(\rho)=\operatorname{tr}\rho$ for all trace-class $\rho$ and $\|\Phi(\rho)\|_1\le\|\rho\|_1$ for self-adjoint $\rho$; it maps density operators to density operators. No claim is made that every positive trace-preserving map has such a family.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a nonzero complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let $(X,\Sigma)$ be a measurable space.

By an **abstract instrument** on $(X,\Sigma)$ we mean a map $J:\Sigma\to\mathcal B(\mathcal S_1(H))$ into bounded linear maps of the trace-class operators such that (i) each $J_B$ is completely positive (every finite amplification $I_{\mathbb C^n}\otimes J_B$ preserves positivity on positive trace-class operators); (ii) for pairwise disjoint $B_n$ with union $B$, $J_B(\rho)=\sum_nJ_{B_n}(\rho)$ in trace norm for every trace-class $\rho$; (iii) $J_X$ is trace preserving.

Then:

(a) for every $B\in\Sigma$ there is a unique effect $F_B\in\mathcal B(H)$, $0\le F_B\le I$ ([[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]]), with $\operatorname{tr}\bigl(J_B(T)\bigr)=\operatorname{tr}(TF_B)$ for every trace-class $T$;

(b) $F_X=I$, and $B\mapsto F_B$ is weakly countably additive: for pairwise disjoint $B_n$ with union $B$ and all $x,y\in H$, $\langle F_Bx,y\rangle=\sum_n\langle F_{B_n}x,y\rangle$;

(c) for every $B$ and every bounded $A\in\mathcal B(H)$ there is a unique bounded operator $J_B^*(A)$ with $\operatorname{tr}\bigl(J_B(T)A\bigr)=\operatorname{tr}\bigl(TJ_B^*(A)\bigr)$ for every trace-class $T$; the map $A\mapsto J_B^*(A)$ is linear and bounded with $J_B^*(A)\ge0$ for $A\ge0$ and $J_X^*(I)=I$;

(d) for a density operator $\rho$ and $B\in\Sigma$ the number $p(B)=\operatorname{tr}(J_B(\rho))=\operatorname{tr}(\rho F_B)$ lies in $[0,1]$, and $p$ is countably additive. Whether an experimental procedure realizes such an instrument is a separate model assumption; the conditional update $J_B(\rho)/p(B)$ is defined only when $p(B)>0$.

No universal representation theorem for arbitrary instruments is asserted.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H,K$ be complex Hilbert spaces with the pairing linear in the first argument ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[def-hilbert-space]]). Write $H\odot K$ for the complex vector space of [[def-tensor-product-of-modules-by-generators-and-relations]] applied to $R=\mathbb C$ and the $\mathbb C$-modules $H,K$: its underlying abelian group is the quotient $F/H_0$ of that definition, the class of $e_{(u,v)}$ is written $u\otimes v$, every element of $H\odot K$ is a finite sum of elementary tensors $u\otimes v$, and the defining relations make $(u,v)\mapsto u\otimes v$ $\mathbb C$-bilinear. Define, for finite sums,

$$\Bigl\langle\sum_i u_i\otimes v_i,\ \sum_j u'_j\otimes v'_j\Bigr\rangle:=\sum_{i,j}\langle u_i,u'_j\rangle_H\,\langle v_i,v'_j\rangle_K .$$

Then:

(a) *well-definedness and sesquilinearity*: the displayed value is independent of the representatives of its two arguments and defines a Hermitian sesquilinear form on $H\odot K$, linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the second;

(b) *positive definiteness and orthonormal coordinates*: $\langle z,z\rangle\ge0$ for every $z\in H\odot K$, with $\langle z,z\rangle=0$ exactly for $z=0$; more precisely, if $U_0\subseteq H$ and $V_0\subseteq K$ are finite-dimensional subspaces with orthonormal bases $e_1,\dots,e_p$ and $f_1,\dots,f_q$, then every $z$ in the span of the elementary tensors from $U_0\times V_0$ has a unique expansion $z=\sum_{a,b}c_{ab}\,e_a\otimes f_b$ and $\langle z,z\rangle=\sum_{a,b}|c_{ab}|^2$; in particular the assignment is an inner product on $H\odot K$;

(c) *completion*: the norm completion $(\widehat{H\odot K},\kappa)$ of $H\odot K$ ([[def-completion-of-a-normed-space]]) exists and, with the unique inner product extending the given one, is a complex Hilbert space; $\kappa$ is a linear isometry with dense image, $\|\kappa(u\otimes v)\|=\|u\|\,\|v\|$, and any two completions of $H\odot K$ are identified by a unique surjective linear isometry fixing $H\odot K$ pointwise;

(d) *product bases*: if $(e_a)_{a\in A}$ and $(f_b)_{b\in B}$ are orthonormal families in $H$ and $K$ whose closed linear spans are $H$ and $K$, then $(\kappa(e_a\otimes f_b))_{(a,b)\in A\times B}$ is an orthonormal family in $\widehat{H\odot K}$ whose closed linear span is $\widehat{H\odot K}$; if $A$ and $B$ are countable then $\widehat{H\odot K}$ is separable.

The notation $H\widehat{\otimes}K:=\widehat{H\odot K}$ is used in the rest of this pair. No assertion is made about unbounded operators or algebraic sums of non-completed tensor products.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space with the pairing linear in the first argument ([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), and for $u,v\in H$ let

$$T_{u,v}x:=\langle x,v\rangle u\qquad(x\in H).$$

Then:

(a) $T_{u,v}\in\mathcal B(H)$ with $\|T_{u,v}\|=\|u\|\,\|v\|$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-operator-norm]]), and the assignment $(u,v)\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is linear in $u$ and conjugate-linear in $v$;

(b) $T_{u,v}$ is trace class ([[def-trace-class-operator]]) with $\|T_{u,v}\|_1=\|u\|\,\|v\|$ and $\operatorname{tr}(T_{u,v})=\langle u,v\rangle$;

(c) $T_{u,v}^*=T_{v,u}$ ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]); $T_{x,x}\ge0$ with $\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,x})=\|x\|^2$ and $\|T_{x,x}\|_1=\|x\|^2$ ([[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]]);

(d) for bounded $B\in\mathcal B(H)$, $BT_{u,v}=T_{Bu,v}$ and $T_{u,v}B=T_{u,B^*v}$;

(e) for trace-class $S$, $ST_{u,v}=T_{Su,v}$, $T_{u,v}S=T_{u,S^*v}$ and $\operatorname{tr}(ST_{x,y})=\langle Sx,y\rangle=\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,y}S)$;

(f) the map $(u,v)\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is continuous $H\times H\to\mathcal S_1(H)$ for the trace norm, so in particular $v\mapsto T_{v,v}$ and $v\mapsto T_{u,v}$ are trace-norm continuous for fixed $u$.

*Scope.* Only the rank-one calculus is asserted; no spectral, compactness or ideal statement beyond (b) is claimed, and no physical interpretation is attached.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice
([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $m_1,m_2>0$, put
$M:=m_1+m_2$, $\mu:=m_1m_2/M$, let $\kappa>0$, $\hbar>0$, and define the
linear coordinate map
$$\Phi:\mathbb R^6\to\mathbb R^6,\qquad \Phi(x_1,x_2):=\Bigl(R,r\Bigr),\qquad R:=\frac{m_1x_1+m_2x_2}{M},\quad r:=x_1-x_2,$$
with inverse $x_1=R+\frac{m_2}{M}r$, $x_2=R-\frac{m_1}{M}r$. Write $L^2=L^2(\mathbb R^6;\mathbb C)$ and for $f\in L^2$ put $(U_\Phi f)(R,r):=f(x_1,x_2)$ with $x_i$ expressed through $(R,r)$. Then:

(a) $\Phi$ is a linear isomorphism with $|\det\Phi|=1$, and $U_\Phi$ is a unitary operator on $L^2$;

(b) for every Schwartz function $f$, $U_\Phi(-(\hbar^2/2m_1)\Delta_{x_1}-(\hbar^2/2m_2)\Delta_{x_2})U_\Phi^{-1}f=(-(\hbar^2/2M)\Delta_R-(\hbar^2/2\mu)\Delta_r)f$; moreover both sides define the same self-adjoint operator on the common domain $H^2(\mathbb R^6;\mathbb C)$;

(c) the operator $H_2:=-(\hbar^2/2m_1)\Delta_{x_1}-(\hbar^2/2m_2)\Delta_{x_2}-\kappa/|x_1-x_2|$ with $D(H_2):=H^2(\mathbb R^6;\mathbb C)$ is self-adjoint, and $U_\Phi H_2U_\Phi^{-1}=T_R\otimes I+I\otimes C_\mu$ under the identification $L^2(\mathbb R^6)\cong L^2(\mathbb R^3_R)\widehat\otimes L^2(\mathbb R^3_r)$ of [[lem-nrqm-composites-euclidean-l2-product-unitary]], where $T_R:=-(\hbar^2/2M)\Delta_R$ and $C_\mu:=-(\hbar^2/2\mu)\Delta_r-\kappa/|\cdot|$;

(d) $\sigma(H_2)=[-\frac{\mu\kappa^2}{2\hbar^2},\infty)$, and $H_2$ has no eigenvalues at all: the negative part of its spectrum is the interval $[-\frac{\mu\kappa^2}{2\hbar^2},0)$, consisting entirely of continuous spectrum, and each relative bound-state energy $-\frac{\mu\kappa^2}{2\hbar^2n^2}$ of $C_\mu$ is a threshold of the continuous spectrum of $H_2$ rather than an eigenvalue; in particular a relative bound state tensored with any centre-of-mass state is not an eigenvector of $H_2$;

(e) the total momentum operators $P_{R,j}:=\frac{\hbar}{i}\partial_{R_j}$ on the maximal domain $D(P_{R,j})=\{g\in L^2(\mathbb R^3_R):\partial_{R_j}g\in L^2\text{ as a weak derivative}\}$ are self-adjoint and have no nonzero eigenvectors in $L^2(\mathbb R^3_R)$; consequently every eigenvector equation for $H_2$ is incompatible with a normalisable state of definite total momentum.

All statements are mathematical statements about the operators on $L^2(\mathbb R^6)$ and the coordinate map; no physical interpretation, no identification of $m_1,m_2,\kappa,\hbar$ with measured constants, and no claim about nature is made.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). For $k\in\mathbb N$ let $\mathcal P_k$ be the real vector space of homogeneous polynomial functions of degree $k$ on $\mathbb R^3$, with $\mathcal P_k=\{0\}$ for $k<0$. Put $r^2=x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2$, $\Delta=\partial_1^2+\partial_2^2+\partial_3^2$, and $\mathcal H_k=\ker(\Delta|_{\mathcal P_k})$. Define their explicit complexifications $\mathcal P_k^{\mathbb C}:=\{p+iq:p,q\in\mathcal P_k\}$ and $\mathcal H_k^{\mathbb C}:=\{p+iq:p,q\in\mathcal H_k\}$. The Laplacian and real rotation pullbacks extend complex-linearly to these spaces. Then:

(a) for every $l\ge2$, $\Delta:\mathcal P_l\to\mathcal P_{l-2}$ is onto and $\mathcal P_l=\mathcal H_l\oplus r^2\mathcal P_{l-2}$ as real vector spaces; the same decomposition holds over $\mathbb C$ after complexification;

(b) for every $l\ge0$, $\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathcal P_l=\binom{l+2}{2}$ and $\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathcal H_l=2l+1$, and these equal $\dim_{\mathbb C}\mathcal P_l^{\mathbb C}$ and $\dim_{\mathbb C}\mathcal H_l^{\mathbb C}$, respectively;

(c) for $l\ge0$ and $0\le m\le l$, using the exact positive constants $b_{lm}$ and Legendre polynomials of [[thm-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-associated-polynomial-normalization]], set, initially for $x\ne0$,
$$h_{lm}(x):=(-1)^m b_{lm}(2\pi)^{-1/2}(x_1+ix_2)^m r^{l-m}P_l^{(m)}(x_3/r).$$
This expression has a unique polynomial extension to $\mathbb R^3$, with value $0$ at the origin for $l>0$ and the nonzero constant $b_{00}/\sqrt{2\pi}=1/\sqrt{4\pi}$ for $l=0$. It is a nonzero element of $\mathcal H_l^{\mathbb C}$, and $h_{lm}|_{S^2}=Y_{lm}$. For $1\le m\le l$ define $h_{l,-m}=(-1)^m\overline{h_{lm}}$. The $2l+1$ functions $(h_{lm})_{-l\le m\le l}$ form a complex basis of $\mathcal H_l^{\mathbb C}$, and their restrictions form a complex basis of its restriction space. The restriction maps on $\mathcal H_l$ and $\mathcal H_l^{\mathbb C}$ are injective; a corresponding real basis of $\mathcal H_l$ is $h_{l0}$ together with $\sqrt2\operatorname{Re}h_{lm}$ and $\sqrt2\operatorname{Im}h_{lm}$ for $1\le m\le l$.

(d) let $\omega,m_0,\hbar>0$, $\ell=\sqrt{\hbar/(m_0\omega)}$, and let $(\psi_N)_{N\in\mathbb N^3}$ be the complex Hilbert-space eigenbasis of the realised isotropic oscillator in [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-multidimensional-oscillator-completeness]], with $H\psi_N=\hbar\omega(|N|+\tfrac32)\psi_N$, $|N|=N_1+N_2+N_3$. Put $E_M:=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb C}\{\psi_N:|N|=M\}$. Then:

(d1) each $f\in E_M$ has a unique representation $f=P e^{-r^2/(2\ell^2)}$ with a complex polynomial $P$ of degree at most $M$, and the map $\Phi_M:E_M\to\mathcal P_M^{\mathbb C}$ assigning its degree-$M$ homogeneous part (zero if its degree is lower) is a complex-linear isomorphism. It restricts to a real-linear isomorphism from the real span of the same $\psi_N$ onto $\mathcal P_M$;

(d2) every rotation $R\in SO(3)$ preserves $E_M$. With $\pi(R)f=f(R^{-1}\cdot)$ on $E_M$ and $\rho(R)p=p(R^{-1}\cdot)$ on $\mathcal P_M^{\mathbb C}$, one has $\Phi_M\pi(R)=\rho(R)\Phi_M$; the same identity holds on the real subspaces;

(d3) one has the decomposition into rotation-invariant complex subspaces
$$E_M=\bigoplus_{j=0}^{\lfloor M/2\rfloor}\Phi_M^{-1}(r^{2j}\mathcal H_{M-2j}^{\mathbb C}),$$
and the analogous real decomposition. The harmonic orbital degrees in this level are exactly $l=M,M-2,M-4,\dots\ge0$, each occurring once as a harmonic-degree summand. In particular not every $l\le M$ occurs, and $\sum_{j=0}^{\lfloor M/2\rfloor}(2(M-2j)+1)=\binom{M+2}{2}$ is the oscillator-level multiplicity.

All assertions concern polynomials and the realised oscillator, with no physical interpretation asserted.
